$resources['igt-workplace'] = [
    'kind' => 'passage', 'title' => 'Reading Section 2 · Workplace procedures and staff development',
    'paragraphs' => [
        'WESTBANK FOOD COOPERATIVE: STARTING A SHIFT — A. All staff must sign the attendance book at reception before entering the stock area. This allows the duty manager to account for everyone during an emergency. Visitors and couriers must remain in the marked delivery bay until a member of staff meets them. Do not invite a driver into the staff canteen while you locate an order. If you are working with a temporary colleague, check that the duty manager knows their name and that an induction has been arranged. Wearing a uniform does not establish that someone has received site instructions.',
        'B. Check incoming goods before moving them into storage. If packaging is damaged, take a photograph of it while it is still on the delivery trolley. Then attach an orange tag and place the affected item in the quarantine area. Do not discard the packaging until the supplier has responded: it may help establish where damage occurred. A cracked container or an unusual smell should be referred to the floor coordinator immediately. Staff must not taste food to decide whether it is safe. An item held for investigation must not be included in a customer order, even if its printed date has not passed.',
        'C. The chilled store must remain at or below five degrees Celsius. Record the display reading at the beginning of each shift, rather than copying the previous entry. An unexpectedly high reading requires a call to the floor coordinator and a check that the door has closed properly. Never reset a warning merely to silence it. Place goods with the earliest use-by dates nearest the front, and leave space around the ventilation grilles. At the end of the shift, complete the stock report, including any adjustments you made. This deadline is important because the next team uses the report when planning deliveries.',
        'D. Vehicle keys are kept in the locked cabinet beside the dispatch desk. Only authorised drivers may remove them, and each collection must be entered in the key register. If you find an unlabelled key, give it to the duty manager instead of testing it in different vehicles. Keep personal bags in the lockers provided so that aisles remain clear. Report a spill promptly and prevent others from entering the affected area; do not begin cleaning an unfamiliar substance without advice. Safety instructions take priority over meeting a delivery target.',
        'STAFF LEARNING PROGRAMME — E. The cooperative offers short courses on stock systems, customer communication and basic supervisory skills. Courses take place during paid working time. Managers should arrange cover so that attendance does not leave a colleague responsible for two work areas. Staff on temporary contracts can apply as well as permanent employees. Places are allocated according to the relevance of a course to current duties or an agreed development plan, not simply to the people who ask first. If several suitable applicants need the same session, the learning coordinator will discuss an additional date with the trainer.',
        'F. Discuss a course with your line manager before sending the application form. The manager confirms whether the proposed date can be covered; the learning coordinator then sends the final booking. You must not exchange places with another employee without approval, because the trainer prepares materials for the stated participants. If you cannot attend, contact the coordinator as soon as possible. There is no charge to staff for cancellation, but early notice allows a waiting colleague to take the place. Missing a session does not remove the need to complete compulsory safety training.',
        'G. After a course, choose one realistic change to try at work and discuss it with your manager. The aim is to use learning rather than collect attendance certificates. Some courses include a practical assessment and award a certificate on successful completion; others provide advice without any formal qualification. Check the course description if certification matters for your role. Four weeks after attendance, the coordinator asks what you have tried and whether you need further support. Honest reports of obstacles are welcome: an employee should not claim a change worked merely to produce a positive evaluation.',
    ],
];
$resources['igt-repair'] = [
    'kind' => 'passage', 'title' => 'Reading Section 3 · The repair shop on the high street',
    'paragraphs' => [
        'A. When a kettle stops working or a jacket loses its zip, replacing it can appear easier than finding somebody to repair it. Yet some town centres are seeing renewed interest in small repair businesses. This interest is sometimes described as nostalgia for a less wasteful past. Nostalgia may attract attention, but it does not explain why a service remains useful. A modern repair shop operates among online retailers, sealed electronic devices and customers with little spare time. To survive, it must offer a dependable answer to a practical question: will the cost, inconvenience and uncertainty of repair be worthwhile compared with replacement? The answer differs greatly between objects and between households.',
        'B. The first barrier is often diagnosis. A customer knows that an object does not work but may not know which component has failed or whether a replacement part exists. Examining it takes skilled time, even when no repair follows. Some shops charge an inspection fee and deduct it from the final bill if the customer proceeds. Others offer a free initial conversation but charge for opening a device. Neither arrangement is automatically fairer: what matters is that the terms are explained before work begins. Clear estimates and permission before additional spending reduce the fear of receiving a bill larger than the object’s value. Trust starts with information, not a promise that everything can be fixed.',
        'C. Access to parts creates a second difficulty. A familiar screw may be easy to obtain, whereas a unique plastic fitting can make a simple-looking repair impossible. Manufacturers sometimes change a component without making the difference obvious on the outside of a product. A repairer then needs an exact model number rather than a photograph of a similar appliance. Keeping large stocks of rarely used parts is expensive, so many shops order them after diagnosis. Customers may interpret this wait as poor service unless the reason is explained. Digital catalogues and shared supplier networks can help, but they cannot create a part that is no longer made or remove restrictions on technical information.',
        'D. Skills are equally important. Repair work combines knowledge of materials with the ability to investigate an unfamiliar fault. A short online video may demonstrate one procedure clearly, but a professional must decide whether that procedure suits the object in front of them. Training therefore needs opportunities to observe judgement as well as repeat a technique. Some independent shops take apprentices, while others cooperate with colleges on supervised projects. In either case, time spent teaching has to fit around paid work. Praising practical skills while expecting experienced workers to pass them on without support is unlikely to build a dependable supply of future repairers.',
        'E. Community repair events perform a related but different role. Volunteers may help an owner mend a bag or identify a loose connection, and the owner can learn by taking part. Such events often serve people who would not initially approach a commercial shop. They can also refer unsafe or complicated jobs to professionals. However, a monthly event cannot promise the opening hours, spare-part supply or consumer guarantees expected from a business. Treating volunteers as a free substitute for professional services risks disappointing customers and undervaluing skilled labour. Cooperation works better when the limits of each service are clear and referrals move in both directions.',
        'F. Local authorities interested in repair can do more than sponsor a publicity day. Flexible leases may allow a small business to test demand without accepting years of rent, and clear directories can make services easier to find. Public purchasing can also reward products with available parts and understandable maintenance instructions. These measures do not mean keeping every object in use indefinitely: a badly damaged or inefficient product may sensibly be replaced. Nor should success be measured only by the weight of material diverted from waste. A repair service can save a household money, preserve a valued possession or develop local expertise. A useful evaluation records both outcomes and limits, rather than counting every repaired object as an identical environmental victory.',
        'G. Customers also influence whether repair becomes an ordinary habit. Keeping model details and purchase records can shorten a diagnosis, while describing how a fault developed is more useful than repeatedly switching a damaged device on. Before buying a replacement, a customer can ask whether an inspection is available and what it costs. This is an opportunity to obtain information, not an obligation to approve work at any price. Equally, a repairer who advises against a job may be acting responsibly rather than refusing to help. A relationship based on clear questions and realistic expectations can make the next decision easier, even when the present object cannot be saved. Over time, the visibility of these everyday exchanges may matter more than a single successful campaign encouraging people to mend something for the first time.',
    ],
];

$choice = static function (string $id, string $task, string $title, string $resource, string $prompt, array $options, string $answer, string $explanation, string $instructions = 'Choose ONE answer.'): array {
    $out = [];
    foreach ($options as $key => $text) $out[] = ['id' => (string) $key, 'text' => $text];
    return ['id' => $id, 'type' => 'choice', 'task_type' => $task, 'title' => $title, 'resource_id' => $resource, 'prompt' => $prompt, 'instructions' => $instructions, 'options' => $out, 'answer' => $answer, 'explanation' => $explanation];
};
$short = static function (string $id, string $title, string $resource, string $prompt, array $answer, string $explanation, int $maxWords = 1): array {
    $limits = [1 => 'Choose ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER from the text.', 2 => 'Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the text.', 3 => 'Choose NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS from the text.', 4 => 'Choose NO MORE THAN FOUR WORDS from the text.'];
    return ['id' => $id, 'type' => 'short', 'task_type' => 'Sentence completion', 'title' => $title, 'resource_id' => $resource, 'prompt' => $prompt, 'instructions' => $limits[$maxWords], 'max_words' => $maxWords, 'answer' => $answer, 'explanation' => $explanation];
};
